      aliases: ["acyclovir valacyclovir famciclovir comparison", "HSV VZV antiviral mechanism map", "viral thymidine kinase drugs", "why acyclovir is selective", "herpes antiviral resistance"],
      class: "Herpesvirus nucleoside-analogue activation, selectivity, and resistance pathway",
      entryType: "drug-class-card", classCard: true, isDrugClassCard: true,
      classExampleNames: ["Acyclovir", "Valacyclovir", "Famciclovir"],
      classPathway: ["Antiviral pharmacology", "HSV and VZV nucleoside analogues", "Viral kinase activation and DNA-polymerase inhibition"],
      usedToTreat: "HSV-1, HSV-2, and varicella-zoster virus infections according to drug, route, severity, immune status, timing, and indication. Oral valacyclovir and famciclovir improve outpatient exposure, while intravenous acyclovir is used when rapid reliable systemic exposure is needed for severe HSV or VZV disease.",
      description: "Acyclovir, valacyclovir, and famciclovir exploit an enzyme that herpes-infected cells make. Viral thymidine kinase performs the first phosphorylation step far more efficiently in infected cells than in uninfected cells. Host kinases then form active triphosphates that inhibit viral DNA polymerase. Valacyclovir is the L-valyl prodrug of acyclovir and mainly solves acyclovir's poor oral bioavailability. Famciclovir is converted to penciclovir, whose triphosphate persists longer inside infected cells but usually slows DNA elongation rather than producing the strict chain termination associated with acyclovir triphosphate. The pathway explains both selectivity and resistance.",
      mechanism: "HSV or VZV thymidine kinase converts acyclovir or penciclovir to a monophosphate; cellular guanylate and nucleoside-diphosphate kinases add the next two phosphates. Acyclovir triphosphate competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate for viral DNA polymerase and, because it lacks a normal 3-prime hydroxyl group, becomes incorporated and terminates or functionally arrests the growing viral DNA chain. Penciclovir triphosphate also competitively inhibits viral polymerase, but its incorporated form is not an obligate immediate chain terminator. Loss or alteration of viral thymidine kinase prevents activation and can cause cross-resistance among acyclovir, valacyclovir, and famciclovir; polymerase mutations provide another route. Foscarnet bypasses phosphorylation and therefore may retain activity against thymidine-kinase-deficient virus, although polymerase resistance can still occur.",
      boxedWarning: "These three agents have no current U.S. boxed warning, but renal and neurologic toxicity can become severe when acyclovir exposure rises. Acyclovir is eliminated mainly by the kidney and can precipitate in renal tubules when intravenous infusion is too rapid, hydration is poor, or dosing is not adjusted for kidney function. Accumulation can produce confusion, tremor, hallucinations, myoclonus, or seizures. Valacyclovir has also been associated with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura/hemolytic uremic syndrome in severely immunocompromised people receiving very high doses. The absence of a box does not remove dose, hydration, or neurologic surveillance.",
