      usedToTreat: "CMV retinitis, tissue-invasive CMV disease, and transplant-related CMV prevention or treatment according to population and protocol. Ganciclovir or oral valganciclovir is usually preferred when susceptible virus and marrow reserve permit; foscarnet or cidofovir is reserved for resistance, intolerance, or selected specialist situations because nephrotoxicity and metabolic toxicity are substantial.",
      description: "CMV therapy is a balance between the same viral endpoint and very different activation and toxicity pathways. Ganciclovir requires the CMV UL97 kinase and then inhibits UL54 DNA polymerase; valganciclovir is its well-absorbed oral prodrug. Foscarnet is a pyrophosphate mimic that directly binds the polymerase pyrophosphate site and needs no viral kinase, so UL97-resistant CMV may remain susceptible. Cidofovir is already a nucleotide monophosphate analogue and needs only host-cell phosphorylation, also bypassing UL97. The price of bypassing activation is not safety: foscarnet can cause renal injury, calcium/magnesium/potassium disturbances, and seizures, while cidofovir concentrates in proximal tubular cells and requires probenecid plus saline.",
      mechanism: "CMV UL97 phosphorylates ganciclovir to its monophosphate; host kinases create ganciclovir triphosphate, which competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate at UL54 viral DNA polymerase and slows or terminates DNA elongation. UL97 mutation commonly creates low-level ganciclovir resistance by reducing activation, while UL54 mutation can create broader resistance involving ganciclovir, cidofovir, or foscarnet. Foscarnet directly and reversibly occupies the pyrophosphate-binding site on viral DNA polymerase, blocking cleavage of pyrophosphate from incoming nucleotides without intracellular activation. Cidofovir enters as a phosphonate nucleotide analogue, is converted by cellular kinases to cidofovir diphosphate, and competes at viral polymerase. Organic-anion transporter uptake into proximal tubules explains cidofovir nephrotoxicity; probenecid blocks that uptake and saline reduces tubular concentration.",
      boxedWarning: "Ganciclovir and valganciclovir carry boxed warnings for severe hematologic toxicity, impaired fertility, fetal toxicity, mutagenicity, and carcinogenic potential. Foscarnet labeling emphasizes dose-limiting renal impairment and seizures related to mineral and electrolyte disturbances. Cidofovir carries boxed warnings for dose-dependent nephrotoxicity, neutropenia, carcinogenicity, teratogenicity, and hypospermia and must be given with probenecid and intravenous saline. These warnings are mechanism-linked: rapidly dividing marrow cells are vulnerable to ganciclovir, while renal tubular handling drives foscarnet and cidofovir injury.",
      adverseEffects: ["Ganciclovir/valganciclovir: neutropenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia, infection, GI effects, renal accumulation, and reproductive or fetal toxicity", "Foscarnet/cidofovir: acute kidney injury, electrolyte depletion, seizures, arrhythmia, genital ulceration, uveitis or ocular hypotony, neutropenia, and infusion toxicity"],
