      name: "Acyclovir", generic: "acyclovir",
      aliases: ["Zovirax", "acyclovir sodium", "aciclovir", "acyclovir IV", "acyclovir tablet", "acyclovr"],
      brandExamples: ["Zovirax"],
      class: "Herpesvirus-selective guanosine nucleoside analogue activated by viral thymidine kinase",
      classPathway: ["Antiviral medication", "HSV and VZV nucleoside analogue", "Viral thymidine kinase activation", "Viral DNA-polymerase inhibition and chain termination"],
      usedToTreat: "HSV-1 and HSV-2 mucocutaneous or genital disease, herpes encephalitis, neonatal HSV, varicella, and herpes zoster according to route and severity. It suppresses active replication but does not eliminate latent virus from sensory ganglia.",
      description: "Acyclovir is a guanosine nucleoside analogue that becomes concentrated pharmacologically inside HSV- or VZV-infected cells because viral thymidine kinase performs its first activation step. The active triphosphate competitively inhibits viral DNA polymerase and terminates the growing viral DNA chain. Oral therapy treats or suppresses selected mucocutaneous disease; intravenous therapy provides reliable exposure for encephalitis, neonatal herpes, disseminated infection, severe varicella-zoster disease, or inability to absorb oral medication. Its selectivity explains why host DNA synthesis is less affected, while renal elimination explains why dehydration or unadjusted dosing can cause crystal nephropathy and neurotoxicity.",
      mechanism: "HSV and VZV thymidine kinase phosphorylates acyclovir to acyclovir monophosphate. Host guanylate kinase and other cellular kinases create acyclovir triphosphate, which has much greater affinity for viral DNA polymerase than for host polymerases. It competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate, is incorporated into viral DNA, and lacks the normal 3-prime hydroxyl group needed to form the next phosphodiester bond. Polymerase stalls after incorporation, stopping genome replication. Viral thymidine-kinase loss or alteration reduces activation and can produce resistance; viral DNA-polymerase mutation can reduce binding. Acyclovir is filtered and secreted by the kidney and is poorly soluble in urine, so high tubular concentration during rapid IV infusion or dehydration can form crystals, obstruct flow, and reduce clearance further.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Renal failure and neurotoxicity can occur, especially with rapid IV infusion, inadequate hydration, pre-existing kidney dysfunction, older age, high exposure, or concurrent nephrotoxins. Infuse IV doses over the labeled interval, maintain hydration, and adjust for renal function. Confusion, hallucinations, tremor, myoclonus, agitation, dysarthria, or seizures can signal drug accumulation rather than worsening encephalitis alone."),
