      nursingEssentials: ["For IV therapy, verify dilution, infusion time, hydration, urine output, creatinine trend, and neurologic status before each dose. A sudden creatinine rise can increase acyclovir exposure and create a self-amplifying toxicity cycle.", "Start promptly in suspected herpes encephalitis or neonatal HSV because ongoing viral replication destroys tissue; do not wait for every confirmatory result when clinical urgency warrants empiric therapy."],
      interactions: ["Other nephrotoxins such as calcineurin inhibitors, amphotericin, or high-risk NSAID exposure can compound kidney injury.", "Probenecid and cimetidine can reduce renal clearance; mycophenolate exposure interactions and additive neurotoxicity deserve review in transplant patients."],
      keyLabs: ["Creatinine/eGFR, urine output, hydration and urinalysis when renal injury is suspected; CBC and liver tests during prolonged or severe-disease therapy", "Neurologic examination, lesion progression, ocular involvement, immune status, treatment timing, adherence, and culture/PCR or resistance evaluation when response is poor"],
      nclexTraps: ["Acyclovir does not eradicate latent HSV or VZV; recurrence remains possible after successful treatment.", "New confusion in a renally impaired patient receiving acyclovir can be drug neurotoxicity, viral CNS disease, or both; check exposure and kidney function urgently."],
      populationRisks: populationRisks("Neonatal and pediatric dosing is weight- and indication-specific; neonatal HSV requires prolonged IV treatment and follow-up rather than an adult oral schedule.", "Age-related renal decline, dehydration, and polypharmacy increase crystal nephropathy and neurotoxicity risk even when serum creatinine looks modest.", "Pregnancy registries have not shown a major teratogenic signal, and acyclovir is commonly used when indicated; it enters breast milk, so assess infant exposure and indication."),
