      mechanism: "Valacyclovir is absorbed through peptide transport pathways and hydrolyzed by valacyclovir hydrolase to acyclovir plus L-valine. Viral thymidine kinase then converts acyclovir to the monophosphate, and host kinases generate acyclovir triphosphate. The triphosphate competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate at viral DNA polymerase, becomes incorporated, and lacks the 3-prime hydroxyl needed for continued chain elongation. This produces functional chain termination and traps polymerase. Viral thymidine-kinase mutation can cause cross-resistance to acyclovir and famciclovir. Kidney elimination of acyclovir means reduced filtration or secretion raises plasma and CNS exposure; adequate hydration lowers urinary concentration and crystal formation but does not replace dose adjustment.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Acute renal failure and central nervous system reactions, including agitation, hallucinations, confusion, delirium, seizures, and encephalopathy, are most likely with renal impairment or excessive dosing. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura/hemolytic uremic syndrome has occurred in advanced HIV disease and transplant recipients receiving very high valacyclovir doses. Stop and evaluate a falling platelet count, hemolysis, neurologic change, or renal decline."),
